.\" ai-tools-providers(5) -- the agent and integration manifests of Agent Tools Restricted.
.\" @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@ is substituted at deploy time (install.sh install_subst and the RPM
.\" %prep), like operator.conf(5). The keys described here are the ones providers.lib.sh and
.\" its consumers read; tests/unit/man.sh keeps this page and the shipped manifests in step.
.TH AI-TOOLS-PROVIDERS 5 "" "ai-tools @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@" "Agent Tools Restricted"
.SH NAME
ai-tools-providers \- agent and integration manifests for Agent Tools Restricted
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BI /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/agents.d/ name .conf
.br
.BI /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/integrations.d/ name .conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
A provider is a package that adds something a sandboxed session can use:
an
.B agent
is a coding assistant the toolchain installs and a session runs,
an
.B integration
is a host toolchain the session is given access to.
Each provider package ships one manifest, named after the provider,
in the directory of its kind.
The base package reads the manifests and never names a provider itself,
so a provider is added by installing its package and removed by erasing it.
.PP
.I name
is the token an operator writes in
.B AI_TOOLS_AGENTS
or
.B AI_TOOLS_INTEGRATIONS
in
.BR operator.conf (5)
to enable the provider.
The two directories share one namespace: a name is unique across both kinds.
Whether a session receives a provider is decided by that file and by the
manifest's
.B default_enable
key, as described under
.B ENABLEMENT
below.
.PP
A manifest is package data.
It is owned by
.B root
at mode
.BR 0644 ,
is replaced on upgrade, and is not edited on a host.
It is parsed and never sourced, so a manifest carries settings and cannot
run code.
A manifest that is not owned by root, that is writable by its group or by
others, or that is a symbolic link, is skipped and the reason is reported on
standard error and in the journal; the same applies to the directory that
holds it, in which case every manifest of that kind is skipped.
.PP
Beside its manifest a provider may ship, under the same
.IR name :
a session-environment fragment, a command domain of
.BR ai-tools-admin (8),
and a rule set for the command filters.
See
.B FILES
for each.
.SH GRAMMAR
One
.I KEY=value
per line, in the grammar
.BR operator.conf (5)
describes under
.BR GRAMMAR :
quotes are optional, list items separate on commas and whitespace, and a
.B #
begins a comment.
A repeated key takes its last value.
.SH ENABLEMENT
When
.B operator.conf
sets the key for a provider's kind, the providers enabled are exactly the
names it lists, and
.B default_enable
is ignored.
When that key is absent, the providers enabled are the installed ones whose
manifest sets
.BR default_enable=yes .
A name listed but not installed is reported and skipped.
When
.B operator.conf
is unreadable, or does not meet the ownership and mode rules above, it is
treated as absent.
.SH KEYS
Every key is optional unless stated.
.SS Both kinds
.TP
.B default_enable
.B yes
or
.BR no .
Whether a session receives this provider when
.B operator.conf
does not list providers of its kind.
A provider that changes what the host exposes beyond the sandbox ships
.BR no ,
and is enabled only by being named.
Default:
.BR no .
.TP
.B admin_summary
The one-line description
.B ai-tools-admin --help
prints beside the command domain this package contributes.
A domain exists because its command is installed, not because this key is
set; a domain without the key is listed without a description.
.SS Agents
.TP
.B npm_package
The npm package the toolchain installs for this agent.
Required for an agent to be provisioned.
.TP
.B launcher
The command name, installed as
.BI /opt/ai-tools/bin/ launcher
and the only name an executable may carry to start a session as this agent.
Required.
.TP
.B display_name
What the launch banner and the session unit call the agent.
.TP
.B handback
.B hooks
when the agent converges file ownership itself, per tool call and per turn.
Any other value, or an absent key, makes the launcher hand the project back
once, when the session ends.
Default: the session-end handback.
.TP
.B config_dir
One directory name under
.BR /opt/ai-tools ,
the agent's own configuration and state.
The agent package owns the directory and the files in it.
.TP
.B skills_dir
Where, relative to
.BR config_dir ,
the agent reads skills.
The shared skills under
.B /opt/ai-tools/skills
are linked there, one link per skill.
Unset for an agent that does not read skills; it is given no links.
.TP
.B subagents_dir
Where, relative to
.BR config_dir ,
the agent reads its delegate definitions.
The shared definitions under
.B /opt/ai-tools/subagents
are linked there the same way.
.TP
.B memory_file
The file name, relative to
.BR config_dir ,
the agent reads as instructions at the start of every session.
The shared orientation text under
.B /opt/ai-tools/orientation
is linked under that name.
Unset for an agent that reads none.
.TP
.B entrypoint_fcontext
A file-context pattern for the binary that starts a session, which the
SELinux confinement keys on.
It is applied as a local rule by
.B ai-tools-admin system entrypoints relabel
and by the automatic relabel after a toolchain update.
The pattern must lie under
.B /opt/ai-tools/.nvm/versions/node/
and may use a character class, but no alternation or group; the label it
receives is fixed and is not declared here.
Without it a session refuses to launch on an enforcing host rather than run
unconfined.
.TP
.B release_manifest_url
The vendor's per-release checksum manifest, with one
.B {version}
slot the installed version fills.
A template without the slot is refused.
.TP
.B release_key
The OpenPGP keyring that signs the release manifest, a file this agent's
package ships.
The verifier reads the key from this file only.
.TP
.B release_fingerprint
The fingerprint the signing key must have.
A list, so a key rotation ships old and new fingerprints for the overlap.
.PP
The three
.B release_*
keys are read together.
An agent declaring none of them is reported as unverified by
.BR "ai-tools --status" ,
which is not a fault unless the operator required verification.
.SS Integrations
.TP
.B build_output_dirs
Directory names, at any depth inside a claimed project, that hold this
toolchain's build output.
A claim and a relabel map every directory of one of these names, and
everything inside it, to the SELinux build-output type, which the
.B buildexec
policy group may grant execute on.
Each name is one plain component (letters, digits, dot, underscore, hyphen).
Read from every installed manifest, enabled or not.
.TP
.B selinux_layout_module
The SELinux policy module that types those same directories at the moment
they are created, and inside sandbox clones.
It holds type transitions and file contexts and does not add any
permission, so it loads with the integration:
.BI "ai-tools-admin " name " bootstrap"
loads it where the policy package is installed, and the policy package loads
it for every installed integration that declares one.
The module must be shipped by the policy package under
.BR /usr/share/selinux/packages/ai-tools/ .
.TP
.B selinux_groups
The optional SELinux policy groups this toolchain needs on an enforcing
host.
None is enabled automatically; each is an explicit
.BR "ai-tools-admin selinux groups enable" .
.B ai-tools --providers
and
.BI "ai-tools-admin " name " status"
name the ones not loaded, with the command that enables them.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/agents.d/
Agent manifests, one per installed agent package.
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/integrations.d/
Integration manifests, one per installed integration package.
.TP
.IB /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/session-env.d/ name .env.sh
The provider's session-environment fragment, sourced by the launcher when the
provider is enabled.
.TP
.IB /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/admin-commands.d/ name
The provider's command domain of
.BR ai-tools-admin (8).
.TP
.IB /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/filters.d/ name .rules
The provider's command-filter rules.
.TP
.I /etc/ai-tools/operator.conf
Where an operator enables providers; see
.BR operator.conf (5).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ai-tools (1),
.BR ai-tools-admin (8),
.BR operator.conf (5)
